---
import Layout from '../layouts/main.astro';
---

<Layout title="About — Querial">
	<section class="py-20 md:py-32">
		<div class="container mx-auto px-4">
			<div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto">
				<div class="text-center space-y-4 mb-12">
					<h1 class="text-4xl md:text-6xl font-bold">
						The idea behind{" "}
						<span class="bg-gradient-to-r from-querial-indigo to-querial-indigo-on-dark bg-clip-text text-transparent">
							Querial
						</span>
					</h1>
					<p class="text-xl text-muted-foreground max-w-2xl mx-auto">
						A control plane for SQL that already has to run on many databases,
						not a new language for writing it.
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="prose prose-lg dark:prose-invert mx-auto max-w-none space-y-6 [&>p]:text-muted-foreground [&>h2]:text-foreground">
					<p>
						Database teams already know SQL. What they usually lack is one
						place to version it, bind it to many targets, and prove which run
						actually happened. Scripts live in shared drives. Migrations live
						in a different tool. Cron lives on a box nobody wants to patch.
						Stored procedures drift per tenant. After a restore, nobody can
						say which Friday script actually applied.
					</p>

					<blockquote class="not-prose my-10 border-l-2 border-querial-indigo pl-6 py-1">
						<p class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-medium text-foreground leading-snug">
							Querial manages SQL rather than replacing it.
						</p>
					</blockquote>

					<h2>Design is not deployment</h2>
					<p>
						A pipeline is a reusable design: steps, migrations, parameters,
						and DAG edges. A published version is immutable. A deployment
						binds that version to an environment kind — Development, Staging,
						or Production — through logical connections whose endpoints are
						encrypted per kind. The same version can run for hundreds of
						tenant databases without copying SQL by hand.
					</p>

					<h2>Execution is target-local</h2>
					<p>
						Steps and migrations run inside each target database. Migration
						history on that database is authoritative — restoring a clone
						does not confuse the control plane into guessing. There are no
						distributed transactions across connections. Large intermediates
						travel as Parquet artifacts, not as messages.
					</p>

					<h2>People are isolated by project</h2>
					<p>
						Platform, team, and project are three layers. Isolation is the
						project: pipelines, connections, agents, keys, and runs belong to
						one project. Developer and DevOps are peer roles, not a ladder.
						Team admin inherits Project admin on every project in that team.
						Platform admin is the instance superuser and the only Identity
						role. Invite creates the login and attaches memberships in the
						same step; accept only sets a password.
					</p>

					<h2>Assistance drafts; humans publish</h2>
					<p>
						SqlAssist can propose SQL against the cached schema and the
						pipeline contract. It never auto-publishes. Embeddings live on
						the control database. A human still owns the SQL that ships.
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="text-center mt-16 pt-12 border-t">
					<h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold mb-4">Read by persona</h2>
					<p class="text-muted-foreground mb-6">
						Guides are split for Developers, DevOps, and Platform, Team, and
						Project admins.
					</p>
					<div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
						<a href="/docs" class="inline-flex items-center justify-center h-10 px-6 rounded-md bg-primary text-primary-foreground font-medium hover:bg-primary/90 transition-colors">
							Open the docs
						</a>
						<a href="/contact" class="inline-flex items-center justify-center h-10 px-6 rounded-md border bg-background hover:bg-accent hover:text-accent-foreground transition-colors">
							Contact
						</a>
					</div>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>
</Layout>
